WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.342
A useful way to think about joins is to separate two ideas: matching and preservation.

00:00:06.342 --> 00:00:06.752
The ON condition controls matching.

00:00:06.752 --> 00:00:13.742
It tells SQL when one row from the first table should be connected with one row from the second table.

00:00:13.742 --> 00:00:17.712
For example, an admission matches a patient when the patient IDs are equal.

00:00:17.712 --> 00:00:21.672
The join type controls preservation.

00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:26.857
It answers a different question: if a row has no match, should that row disappear,

00:00:26.857 --> 00:00:30.417
or should it remain with NULL values from the missing side?

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:36.402
This is why the same ON condition can produce different results under INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN.

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:40.832
Imagine an online store. One table contains all customers and another contains orders.

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:46.032
If we use INNER JOIN, customers without orders disappear.

00:00:46.032 --> 00:00:50.047
If we use LEFT JOIN with customers on the left, every customer stays,

00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:52.592
and customers without orders have NULL values for order columns.

00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:55.732
The matching rule did not change.

00:00:55.732 --> 00:01:01.617
The preservation rule changed. Also remember that joins combine rows, not just unique IDs.

00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:06.207
If one customer has three orders, there are three valid customer-order combinations.

00:01:06.207 --> 00:01:11.257
Before you run a join, try to describe it in plain language: compare these two row sets,

00:01:11.257 --> 00:01:16.672
match them using this rule, and preserve these unmatched rows.

00:01:16.672 --> 00:01:19.920
If you can say that clearly, your SQL is usually much easier to design.
